  if (typeof GUIDE_PROFILES !== 'undefined' && GUIDE_PROFILES.marion_brenay) {
    GUIDE_PROFILES.marion_brenay.birthPlace='Saginaw, Saginaw County, Michigan';
    GUIDE_PROFILES.marion_brenay.life="Marion Beulah Brenay was born on 20 January 1923 in Saginaw, Michigan, the second of thirteen children of Charles Albert Brenay and Marian Beulah Skinner. She married James Wilford Webb on 5 May 1941 in St. Johns, Arizona, and became part of the Webb household in the northern Arizona communities where the family worked, traveled, and raised children.\n\nMarion’s life was shaped by movement and responsibility. During the Second World War, when Jay entered Army service, she carried the household through separation, limited money, and the uncertainty of wartime life. Family testimony remembers a modest home near the mill, severe winter weather, and the care required to raise young children in a timber-town landscape.\n\nThe family record names James Sheldon Webb and Jamar Webb among Marion and Jay’s children. Photographs and family documents place Marion in the home, at reunions, during anniversaries, and alongside the growing Webb family. Other memories preserve letters, ration-book material, flowers, household scenes, and gatherings that show the domestic world she helped maintain across the Arizona years.\n\nMarion’s story is also one of family memory. The surviving collection includes childhood, courtship, household, correspondence, military-family, anniversary, and reunion material. These photographs and captions preserve how relatives remembered her, while the 5 May 1941 marriage index provides the clearest direct record of her union with Jay.\n\nHer life connects the Brenay family’s Michigan beginnings to the Webb family’s Arizona home and to the later generations descended through James Sheldon Webb. The family record is extensive, but individual captions and relationships remain carefully distinguished from independent civil records.";
  }

