    GUIDE_PROFILES.jonathan_henry.life='Jonathan Henry Webb was born on 21 June 1886 in Woodruff, Apache County, Arizona, during the territory’s formative years. He was the son of Edward Milo Webb Jr. and Sarah Elizabeth Carling. His childhood belonged to the small agricultural settlements around the Little Colorado, where distance, irrigation, weather, and family cooperation shaped daily life.\n\nOn 16 August 1908, he married Della Ray in Colonia Morelos, in the Agua Prieta region of Sonora, Mexico. They raised ten children, including James Wilford “Jay” Webb, born in 1924. Their household joined the Arizona settlements to the agricultural colonies of northern Mexico, part of a family network that extended across the border and later took root again in the high-country communities of northeastern Arizona.\n\nJonathan and Della’s life crossed Arizona statehood, the Mexican Revolution’s aftermath, two world wars, and the Great Depression. The family’s later years were rooted in the White Mountains, where timber, small farms, close kinship, and long distances shaped everyday life. Della died in 1969 in McNary, Arizona; Jonathan died in 1975 in Lakeside, Arizona, and was buried at Lakeside Cemetery. Through Jay, Jonathan became a direct ancestor of James Sheldon Webb, linking the family’s territorial Arizona beginnings to its modern generations.\n';
