    GUIDE_PROFILES.inez_karen.life=`Inez Karen Prather appears in the Atlas through a clear marriage record, family photographs, and her relationship to James Sheldon Webb. Her standalone public identity record has not been resolved, so this biography intentionally does not assign a birth date, parents, FamilySearch ID, or more specific birthplace than the evidence supports. The page is a documented family-history entry, not a guessed merge with a living or private person.

The strongest anchor is the Arizona county marriage evidence attached to Sheldon’s FamilySearch page. The source is identified as Marriage Affidavits, 1962–1966; the visible record names Inez Karen Prather, gives a 15 January 1965 license date, and places the license in Navajo County. The record’s displayed marriage timing is about 1964, while the license date is 1965. The Atlas preserves both pieces of metadata rather than silently choosing one. The civil record establishes the relationship; it does not by itself establish Inez’s birth, parentage, residence history, or later life.

Family photographs make the young household more visible. An original wedding photograph is captioned 12 June 1965 and places Inez and Sheldon in their Mesa, Arizona marriage context. A companion reception image preserves the family gathering around the event. A separate family document, retained under Documents rather than Photos, provides announcement-style marriage context while remaining distinct from the county record. These items are useful because they show how relatives remembered the event, but they are not treated as substitutes for the civil evidence.

A later photograph identifies Inez with Sheldon and their first child, Shared, around 1968. That image moves the record beyond the wedding and into the ordinary life of a young family. Another family photograph, captioned as a visit after a baby was born around 1978–79, extends the visible timeline into a later generation. The Atlas preserves these images as family testimony, retaining their captions and approximate dates without inventing an exact event chronology or naming relatives who are not securely identified in the attached record.

Inez’s documented relationship also connects her to the broader Webb family page. Through Sheldon, she appears alongside the Arizona childhood, Air Force, Northern Arizona University, and later family memories that define the surrounding household. The Atlas does not make those facts into claims about Inez’s own education, employment, religious life, or birthplace. Instead, it uses the shared photographs and the marriage record to show the household she joined and the family settings in which she is visible.

The page deliberately keeps religious emphasis subordinate to the family record. The 1965 wedding photograph is described as a family image from the Mesa setting, while later household photographs are presented as family-history evidence. No post-1950 religious document is promoted as a central biographical source unless it directly clarifies a genealogical relationship. This keeps the page aligned with the contract while preserving the photographs that genuinely add family context.

The present evidence supports a careful portrait of Inez as Sheldon Webb’s wife, a participant in the young Webb household, and a mother visible with Shared in the family’s preserved photographs. It does not yet support a complete standalone life history. The next responsible research target is the underlying county marriage image and, separately, a source that can establish Inez’s birth and parentage without relying on a guessed public profile.`;
