    GUIDE_PROFILES.jean_baptiste_tellier_candidate.life="Jean Baptiste Tellier dit Benjamin was born about 1781 at Saint-Ours, in the Richelieu valley of Lower Canada. He was the son of Jean-Benigne Benoni Le Tellier dit LaFortune and Marie Lauside Osithe Maillot-Boisclair. His early life belonged to the parish communities of the lower Richelieu, where extended families, church records, farming, and movement among neighboring settlements shaped the rhythm of daily life.\n\nThe surviving family record places Jean-Baptiste in Saint-Ours as an adult. On 28 January 1822, he married Marie-Élisabeth Béfort Philippin there. Their household included nine children, among them Louise-Anne Tellier, born in 1828. Through Louise-Anne, the family record reaches the 1846 marriage at Saint-Michel-de-Sherbrooke, where she married Pierre Goulet. That later marriage documents Louise-Anne’s family connection; it does not by itself resolve the separate question of whether the Goulet household connects to the later Gooley line.\n\nJean-Baptiste’s life was brief by modern standards. He died at Saint-Ours on 24 June 1833, leaving a young family whose later records carry the Tellier name into the wider Quebec landscape. The parish setting remained central: Saint-Ours and the Richelieu communities around it linked household, work, marriage, and remembrance across generations. The family’s movement toward Sorel and other nearby places reflects the ordinary circulation of nineteenth-century Quebec families rather than a single dramatic migration story.\n\nThe record also preserves an earlier marriage entry to Marie Rocbrune. Its chronology is not fully reconciled with the 1822 marriage and the later children, so the page keeps that entry visible without turning it into a confident narrative. The stronger documented thread is the Saint-Ours marriage to Marie-Élisabeth and the nine-child household associated with it.\n\nJean-Baptiste’s story is therefore best understood through the family he established and the parish landscape that preserved it. He was a Saint-Ours-born father in the Tellier dit Benjamin line, whose daughter Louise-Anne carried the family into the 1846 Sherbrooke-area record. The evidence supports that family connection while leaving the larger Goulet–Gooley bridge open for further research.";
